  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v2.5.0: Rate limiting. UseRateLimiting() applies a token-bucket allowance per client IP across HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, returning 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After and a ProblemDetails body. Applied before routing, so static files, the OpenAPI document, and floods at nonexistent paths are covered. [RateLimit(PermitLimit, WindowSeconds)] gives an endpoint its own tighter allowance and [DisableRateLimit] exempts one entirely. X-Forwarded-For is honored only from configured trusted proxies, walking the chain right-to-left. Tracked clients are capped so rotating source addresses cannot exhaust memory. Also adds HttpRequest.RemoteIpAddress and 429 to the cached status lines.\n\nv2.4.0: OpenAPI/Swagger integration. UseOpenApi() serves an OpenAPI 3.0.3 document at /openapi/v1.json and Swagger UI at /swagger, generated once at startup from the route table and served from a pre-built buffer, so there is no per-request cost. Routes, path parameter types, request bodies, response schemas, and content types are inferred from existing metadata; [OpenApiOperation], [OpenApiResponse], [OpenApiParameter], and [OpenApiIgnore] add what cannot be. Schema property names follow the server's JsonSerializerOptions and DataAnnotations become schema constraints. Security schemes match the built-in JWT and API-key handlers. Also adds Router.GetRegisteredRoutes() and EndpointInvoker.ResponseType.\n\nv2.3.0: Response caching. UseResponseCaching() serves repeat GET/HEAD requests from a bounded in-process store, skipping endpoint execution and JSON serialization, and emits Cache-Control/Vary/Age for client and proxy caches. Opt-in per endpoint via [ResponseCache(Duration, Location, VaryByHeader, VaryByQueryKeys)]. Entries expire on their duration and are evicted when a successful POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE hits the same path. Cache hits answer If-None-Match with 304 on HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3. Requests carrying Authorization bypass the shared cache unless the endpoint opts in. Also fixes HTTP/2 outbound flow control: the send path now honors the peer's stream- and connection-level send windows (RFC 7540 6.9) instead of queueing whole bodies at once, and the connection send window starts at the RFC default of 65535 with the client's SETTINGS_INITIAL_WINDOW_SIZE tracked separately.",
